& [HistoricalFinding] is bounded
  + early Simula work belongs to the early 1960s and the user's 1963 label is preserved as received
  + Simula I became the simulation-oriented predecessor while Simula 67 generalized objects, classes, references, and quasi-parallel sequencing
  + Standard Simula defines a quasi-parallel system with at most one operating component at a time and retains reactivation state for other components
  + the Simulation system class provides discrete-event process concepts, ordering, passivation, and reactivation
  + historical correspondence is conceptual evidence and not proof that vintage Simula is fit for Cantor production use

& [HighValueCorrespondence] is strong
  + Simula object or process identity maps to a durable Cantor procedure instance, lane cursor, or faculty-process identity
  + process-local attributes map to versioned explicit procedure state
  + event notice and simulation time map to logical TimeExpression, Calendar candidate, and LaneMessage records
  + detach maps to YIELD with a serialized continuation contract
  + resume or reactivate maps to exact wake and reentry against a pinned generation
  + quasi-parallel sequencing maps to several retained process frames with one admitted operating transition at a time
  + class or prefix specialization maps to reusable process contracts only when identity and inherited authority remain explicit

& [StoredProcedureArchitectureCandidate] is realistic
  + [ProcedureCandidate] contains source, author, purpose, input schema, output schema, declared state, event vocabulary, resource budget, effect class, and provenance
  + [ProcedureIR] is deterministic, versioned, inspectable, and independent of one model provider
  + [ValidationReceipt] records syntax, type, lifecycle, termination, resource, authority, disclosure, determinism, and prohibited-operation checks
  + [CompiledProcedure] binds exact source digest, compiler identity, target profile, bytecode or WASM digest, validation receipt, and correspondence proof
  + [ProcedureAdmission] is an Observer disposition distinct from generation or compilation
  + [InvocationRequest] binds admitted procedure identity and version, typed input, caller, authority, logical-time budget, resource budget, and expected result schema
  + [InvocationResult] returns typed output, trace summary, consumed budget, faults, residuals, and proof without claiming model truth
  + a content-addressed signed procedure catalogue behaves like a semantic stored-procedure registry

& [ModelToolLoop] requires multiple inference passes
  + pass one may gather context and propose a procedure or invocation
  + the controller pauses generation at the structured tool boundary
  + Cantor validates, compiles or resolves, admits when separately authorized, and invokes inside a deterministic sandbox
  + the controller returns a structured tool result as new model input
  + a later inference pass interprets the result and continues
  + ordinary external function calling is sufficient for the first experiment
  + llama.cpp modification is unnecessary unless measured evidence shows the stop-tool-resume boundary is too coarse
  - never: describe the tool result as direct mutation of K/V cache, hidden activation, weights, or one uninterrupted forward pass

& [ModelAuthoredProcedureBoundary] is mandatory
  + a model may author only a ProcedureCandidate by default
  + validation, compilation, admission, storage, invocation, effect mediation, and revocation are separate authorities
  + generated source cannot self-sign, self-admit, expand its input scope, grant capabilities, or invoke itself merely because it compiles
  + new or changed procedures remain sandboxed and effectless until exact policy permits a later phase
  + every invocation pins procedure version, input identity, caller, budget, and deterministic fallback

& [AdoptedAndRejectedSemantics] keeps the useful core
  + [adopt_candidate] process identity, logical event ordering, passivate, detach, resume, reactivate, retained continuation, bounded event queues, and readable model representation
  + [adapt_candidate] class specialization becomes typed composition or explicit contract extension rather than ambient inherited authority
  + [reject_candidate] shared mutable canonical objects, unrestricted inheritance, implicit global simulation state, unbounded event creation, direct I/O, hidden scheduling, and self-modifying admitted procedures
  + [preserve] immutable messages, source lineage, typed effects, authority separation, Observer admission, deterministic replay, and explicit residual uncertainty

& [PowerAssessment] is qualified_high
  + strongest uses are counterfactual planning, temporal simulation, faculty-process coordination, protocol testing, task reentry, and deterministic inference subroutines
  + expected benefit is moving repeated reasoning patterns from prose into inspectable executable contracts
  + main risks are generated-code error, nontermination, resource exhaustion, covert data flow, confused authority, prompt-sourced injection, and procedure-catalogue drift
  + power depends more on the type, effect, admission, and proof system than on Simula syntax itself

& [CandidateLanguageIdentity] remains unresolved
  + possible identities are SOP-S, Cantor Process Procedure, or Simula-inspired SOP process profile
  + vintage Simula source compatibility is not currently a goal
  + bytecode, WASM, Rust lowering, or interpreter target remains unselected
  + a later SJS must decide whether this is a language profile, compiler target, tool protocol, procedure registry, or coordinated combination

& [FirstExperimentCandidate] is deferred
  + define one effectless two-process logical-time procedure with typed input and output
  + demonstrate detach, event notice, reactivation, bounded termination, normalized trace, replay, stale-version refusal, and resource-budget refusal
  + compare hand-authored and model-proposed ProcedureCandidate forms through the same validator
  + permit no filesystem, network, clock, provider, database, Git, calendar synchronization, notification, model mutation, or external effect
  + require separate source preservation and SJS before implementation

& [FPGALanguageFinding] separates semantics from realization
  + VHDL, Verilog, and SystemVerilog are conventional register-transfer-level hardware description languages
  + C or C++ high-level synthesis can lower a bounded algorithm into RTL, but synthesizable code excludes ordinary operating-system behavior and dynamic allocation
  + CIRCT Handshake is an especially relevant intermediate representation because it models independent dataflow processes communicating through FIFO channels
  + these toolchain observations are implementation evidence rather than Cantor authority
  + FPGA languages are better than Simula for expressing circuits, parallel datapaths, registers, memories, clocks, and handshakes
  + FPGA languages are worse than a Simula-inspired SOP profile for expressing named faculty identity, semantic purpose, authority, negotiation, suspension, reentry, and proof obligations

& [PortableLoweringCandidate] preserves one meaning across targets
  + SOP declares semantic anchors, purposes, authority, and contracts
  + a Simula-inspired Cantor Process Procedure surface declares typed processes, messages, logical events, passivation, reactivation, joins, and bounded procedures
  + a canonical typed process IR removes source-language and model-provider ambiguity
  + the software backend may interpret the IR or lower it to WASM or native code
  + an FPGA backend may lower an eligible effectless bounded subset through dataflow IR, HLS, or generated SystemVerilog
  + backend correspondence proof must show that software and hardware executions preserve the same normalized trace and result
  - never: make vintage Simula syntax the FPGA instruction set or let one vendor's HDL define Cantor semantics

& [FPGAEligibleKernelBoundary] is deliberately narrow
  + strong candidates include digest and CRC pipelines, signed-catalogue lookup, relation filtering, bounded graph-frontier expansion, score accumulation, deterministic arbitration, FIFO messaging, barrier accounting, and trace emission
  + the host controller retains model calls, text generation, file and network access, procedure admission, policy decisions, calendar integration, and human-facing explanations
  + multi-model shared inference is coordinated as typed asynchronous tool turns around pinned SOP identities rather than as literal shared hidden state
  + the FPGA may accelerate the common Cantor junction while every model remains an explicitly named participant with its own context and messages
  + hardware compilation is justified only after profiling identifies a stable kernel whose latency, throughput, determinism, power, or isolation benefit exceeds software complexity

& [Disposition] preserves momentum without scope leakage
  + [conceptual_fit] is high
  + [direct_vintage_language_fit] is low_to_medium
  + [Simula_derived_SOP_profile_fit] is high
  + [FPGA_as_process_language_fit] is low
  + [FPGA_as_verified_backend_fit] is medium_to_high_after_profiling
  + [realism] is feasible_with_existing_tool_calling_and_multiple_inference_passes
  + [current_authority] is none
  + [current_goal_effect] is no_change_to_WS-CTPR-010
  + [next] is revisit after the pure CTPR form ABI and deterministic tool boundary are proved
